  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Monday)\nIssued at 1239 PM CDT Tue Sep 29 2026\n\nAnother sunny day with low humidity was underway across the\nMidsouth. An upper level ridge axis was evident on GOES water\nvapor imagery, extending from south TX through the mid-MS River\nValley. Extensive mid and high level cloudiness was spilling over\nthis ridge axis. These clouds were associated with a plume of\ntropical moisture, downstream from a southern branch upper low\ncentered along the CA/AZ border, and Hurricane Polo making\nlandfall in northwest Mexico.\n\nAs we move through Wednesday, the upper low will entrain moisture\nand energy from the remnants of Hurricane Polo, while lifting\neastward to NM. The Midsouth will remain under the western\nperiphery of the upper ridge early in the day. As the upper ridge\nlifts east, southerly return flow will strengthen, while high\nclouds likely increase. 12Z HREF depicts 60F 2 meter dewpoints\nwarming to the 50s, roughly 10 to 15 degrees warmer than today.\nWith warmer afternoon temperatures, the net result will be a\nmodest increase in afternoon relative humidity levels.Wildland\nfire danger along and west of the MS River will be tempered by\nhigher dewpoints and increased high clouds.\n\nGlobal model consensus depicts the upper low becoming positively-\ntilted and weakening over the central plains on Thursday. As the\nremnant upper trough translates eastward on Friday,  a right\nentrance region of an upper jet max will set-up over the\nMidsouth. This will support strengthening southerly return flow\nand increasing PWAT, ranging from 1.75 inches over northeast AR,\nto 2.25 inches around Tupelo, MS Friday morning. This is near the\n99th percentile of climatology. 00Z LFEF depicts persistent weak\n925-850mb theta-e convergence Friday afternoon through Saturday\nevening, along a slow southward-advancing frontal zone. This low\nlevel convergent zone will drop south of our north MS forecast\nzones by 12Z Sunday. Sunday may see a few lingering showers south\nof I-40, aided by modest midlevel lift associated with the\ndeparting upper jet core. Overall storm total QPF has come down\nslightly from yesterday, with amounts ranging from 1.7 inches\nnear Corning, AR to around 3.5 inches around Aberdeen, MS.\n\nDry conditions appear in store for early next week, with highs\nmainly in the 70s and lows in the 50s.",
